          "en": "Two numbers explain the corridor. The river leaves the Himalaya at Haridwar about 250 m above sea level and reaches the delta at sea level, so over roughly 1,500 km its average gradient is about 1 in 6,000 — nearly flat. And it arrives carrying sediment eroded from the fastest-rising mountain range on earth. A heavily loaded river on a nearly flat surface cannot hold one channel: it deposits, its bed rises, it breaches its banks and finds a steeper path, and the channel moves. That single mechanism produces almost everything distinctive about this region. It produces the alluvial plain and its fertile soils, which produced the agricultural surplus that made cities and states possible from around 600 BCE. It produces the huge alluvial fans of the Gandak and the Kosi. It produces the floods that gave the Damodar the name 'Sorrow of Bengal'. It produces the delta, which is still building and still shifting — which is why Tamralipta, once a working seaport, is now inland. And it explains why Pataliputra declined: a city built to control a river junction is exposed if the junction moves.",
